WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.950
In this video, you use Rancher to provision an RKE2 Kubernetes cluster on SUSE Virtualization. Rancher creates the node virtual machines for you.

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:17.590
In Rancher, open Cluster Management and select Create.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:22.480
Select Harvester, the provider for SUSE Virtualization.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:30.060
Choose the cloud credential for the SUSE Virtualization cluster that hosts the nodes, here far-edge.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.310
Name the cluster.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:42.630
One machine pool with one machine runs etcd, the control plane, and workloads. That is enough for a test cluster.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.580
Give each machine four CPUs,

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.170
and eight gigabytes of memory.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:54.080
Choose the namespace for the virtual machines on SUSE Virtualization,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.140
and the default user of the cloud image.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.960
Pick an Ubuntu cloud image that exists on the cluster,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.110
and set the root disk to twenty gigabytes.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:10.430
Attach the machines to a VM network that can reach Rancher.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:17.880
Under Cluster Configuration, keep the default RKE2 version and the Harvester cloud provider.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.280
Select Create.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:26.460
The cluster appears in the list. Rancher starts provisioning it.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:34.900
On far-edge, SUSE Virtualization now runs the node virtual machine that Rancher created.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:43.642
Rancher keeps provisioning the cluster until it is Active. You manage it from Cluster Management like any other cluster.
